      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tifton was founded in 1872 by sawmill owner Henry H. Tift at the junction of two railroads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tifton%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1905, Tifton became the county seat of newly formed Tift County, cementing its regional importance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tifton%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Georgia Museum of Agriculture & Historic Village, formerly Agrirama, sits in Tifton and showcases life from the 1870s to 1910s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tifton%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Tifton once housed the world's second‑largest magnolia tree in Magnolia Tree Park, a living landmark until recently.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tifton%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Coastal Plain Research Arboretum and Abraham Baldwin Agricultural College are key educational and research hubs located in Tifton.",
